    "description": "Consider two integer linear recurrences (a_n) and (b_m) whose characteristic polynomials are reverses of each other, each with a single dominant root of modulus greater than one. The number of common values a_n = b_m exceeding a threshold C is conjecturally at most two once C is large enough. Formalize the transcendence-theoretic step: derive an explicit lower bound for the linear form |n log alpha - m log beta| from Baker's theorem, combine it with the elementary upper bound coming from a_n = b_m, and conclude that only finitely many (n,m) survive with an effectively computable bound on max(n,m). This supplies the currently-hypothesized value bound feeding the proven index reduction.",
